-## 4. Environment specular is computed and thrown away
+### The gloss / metal curves — added 2026-09-08
+
+With **GMM curves (Tank Exporter)** on (`GMM_CURVE`, default on, persisted as
+`gmm_curve`) the PBR path reads a model's map through the curves the owner
+settled on in Tank Exporter:
+
+```
+gloss = pow(R / 0.8, 7)                 floored at 0.02
+metal = min(pow(G / 0.5, 5) * 1.5, 1)
+```
+
+Both slow-then-fast: they crush the low end and pull the top out, which is
+what makes the dynamic range of these maps show - the owner's finding, judged
+"better" on the monastery at first look. The game does the same in kind for
+metal: GB0.x is gamma-encoded and decoded with a pow, and its default metal of
+0.231 - the green channel sits at 60/255 on plain surfaces in every monastery
+map - only lands near a dielectric after such a curve. Without one every
+default surface carried metal 0.23 and read as chrome.
+
+Models only; terrain packs its own values into these channels. Off is the
+linear reading, for the A/B. The energy conservation and the specular block
+read the curved values through one pair (`pbr_gloss` / `pbr_metal`), so they
+cannot disagree.
+
+Checked while settling this: the blue channel is all zero in every one of the
+18 gloss/metal maps and the one metal/AO atlas in the monastery package. The
+building maps average 0.35 to 0.42 in red, so with the 0.8 pivot most plaster
+and stone sits at the gloss floor and only the bright-red details come up
+glossy.
+
+## 4. Environment specular — connected 2026-09-08
+
+**Status.** The term below is now added to the output when `pbr_spec = 1`,
+scaled by the `Env specular` slider (`ENV_SPEC`, per map, 0 = off). The rest
+of this section is the history and the trap it hid; read it, because the
+premise it argues from was wrong and the fix was the opposite of what it says.
+
+What actually happened, measured at one camera with `freezefx` and a
+zero-pixel null control on both builds:
+
+* The cube nuTerra loads **is** the game's `probes/global/pmrem.dds`
+  (`TerrainBuilder.vb`), DXT5, 6 faces, 8 mips, with the HDR exponent in
+  alpha - the monastery one spans alpha 25..255 over 231 values. It was never
+  "not PMREM-encoded"; it was being read through `SRGBtoLINEAR`. Decoded the
+  game's way (`pmrem_decode`, `rgb² · 2^(9a) / 8`) its mean is 2.8 against
+  0.64 read as sRGB.
+* Connected raw, the frame went 116 levels brighter on average - cobbles at
+  199, a white-out. Two corrections, both in the term: the cube takes the same
+  `props.AMBIENT` scale the probe takes, since both are baked in the same HDR
+  units; and F0 is `specTint * metal`, as the sun term builds it, so a
+  dielectric reflects only through the LUT bias. The transcript in
+  GAME_LIGHTING_MODEL §3 writes `specTint * brdf.x` with no metal, taken as an
+  omission - **REASONED**, not read off the disassembly. With both, the lift
+  is +12 levels mean: cobbles +17 to +20, milk cans +19, the matte wall +4.
+* The direction goes through `invView` with no handedness flip, as the
+  pooled-water path does. The legacy path still samples in view space.
+* Occluded by the luminance ratio of the local probe to the global one where
+  the field exists, and by the model AO through the ambient's curve. Faded
+  under standing water like the sun lobe. Not gated on direct light.
+
+Still reading the cube as sRGB: the wet, water and pooled-water taps, whose
+gains were tuned against that reading. Moving them to `pmrem_decode` is a
+separate, measured change.
+
+### History: why it was disconnected
 
